WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
The way I dealt with this, which kind of actually worked in this case, and I still don't know exactly why

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Is that I created something called a Wi-Fi wall of shame, which is basically, I said to the developers, okay, here's a wall

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Every time you have any problem with Wi-Fi

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
write that the time… the date and time you had the problem with the Wi-Fi and stick it on the wall.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.000
And

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Within a couple of days, this wall was completely creaking with

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Post-its saying that kind of like, you know, I had problems with the Wi-Fi, I had difficulty with the Wi-Fi, etc.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.000
We'd been kind of reporting through all of the normal channels to

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.000
To kind of say, look, you can't really do this project

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Unless you have a connection to the Internet, that's pretty basic. And we got nowhere

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
But this kind of way

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Which I know there's a quote from Jerry Springer, which I'm kind of like ambivalent about as a scrum master, but I think I think it might be right. It might be good guidances. He says, I don't solve people's problems. I televise them

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:22.000
And I think that's kind of what I was doing here. I wasn't solving the problem because I tried to solve the problem and completely failed. So I ended up just televising it, ended up shouting about it

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.000
As loud as I could. And mysteriously, it got fixed. I still don't know what happened to fix it

